Due to this problem of overflow, fixing the height of elements with lengths or percentages is something we need to do very carefully on the web.

In many ways, percentages act like length units, and as we [discussed in the lesson on values and units](/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/Styling_basics/Values_and_units#percentages), they can often be used interchangeably with lengths. When using a percentage, you need to be aware of what it is a percentage _of_. In the case of a box inside another container, if you give the child box a percentage width, it will be a percentage of the width of the parent container.

```html live-sample___percent-width

 I have a percentage width. 

```

```css live-sample___percent-width
body {
 font: 1.2em sans-serif;
}

.box {
 border: 5px solid darkblue;
 width: 50%;
}
```

This is because percentages resolve against the size of the containing block. With no percentage applied, our `box` ` ` takes up `100%` of the available space, as it is a block-level element. If we give it a percentage width, this becomes a percentage of the space it would normally fill.

Try editing the above example:

1. Remove the `box` ` `'s `width` declaration to verify that it takes up `100%` of the available `width` by default.
2. Revert your previous change — give the `box` ` ` a `width` of `50%` again.
3. Now give the `container` ` ` a `width` of `50%`. You'll see that the `box` ` `'s `width` gets smaller, because it is relative to the `width` of its container.

If you set `margins` and `padding` as a percentage, you may notice some strange behavior.

In the below example, we have a box, on which we've set a of 10% and a of `10%`. The padding and margin on the top and bottom of the box are the same size as the padding and margin on the left and right.

```html live-sample___percent-mp
 I have margin and padding set to 10% on all sides. 
```

```css live-sample___percent-mp
body {
 font: 1.2em sans-serif;
}
.box {
 border: 5px solid darkblue;
 width: 200px;
 margin: 10%;
 padding: 10%;
}
```

You might expect the percentage top and bottom margins to be a percentage of the element's height, and the percentage left and right margins to be a percentage of the element's width. However, this is not the case!

When you use margin and padding set in percentages, the value is calculated from the **inline size** of the containing block — therefore, the width when working in a horizontal language. In our example, all of the margins and padding are `10%` of the width. This means you can have equal-sized margins and padding all around the box. This is a fact worth remembering if you use percentages in this way.

In addition to giving things a fixed size, we can ask CSS to give an element a minimum or a maximum size. If you have a box that might contain a variable amount of content, and you always want it to be _at least_ a certain height, you could set the property on it. The box will always be at least this height, but will then grow taller if there is more content than the box has space for at its minimum height.

In the next example, you can see two boxes, both with a defined `min-height` of 100 pixels. The box on the left is 100 pixels tall; the box on the right has content that needs more room, and so it has grown taller than 100 pixels.

```html live-sample___min-height



